<?xml version="1.0"?>
<model>
  <name>purple_target</name>
  <version>1.0</version>
  <sdf version='1.5'>model.sdf</sdf>

  <author>
    <name>Roman Bapst (original) / bumblebee purple target replica</name>
    <email>roman@px4.io</email>
  </author>

  <description>
    Purple target aircraft, copied from emir-gazebo-plane2 in iq_sim.
The airframe, wing, and control-surface visuals use Gazebo/Purple
(RGB 1 0 1, OpenCV HSV H=150).

The red HSV detector produced false detections from the sky horizon band,
rendered near BGR (127,127,255), across the full 1920-pixel width during
banked flight. Ground textures also produced red line artifacts at grazing
angles. Purple allows the sky, runway, and grass to remain in the scene.
Measured background coverage was 0.0012 percent for H 140-160, compared
with 1.9 percent for the red window.

Physics, mass, inertia, LiftDrag panels, ArduPilotPlugin channels, ports
9012/9013, and the internal model name emir-gazebo-plane2 remain identical
to the source model. Scoped joint and IMU names depend on this name.
Changing it prevents the plugins from locating the joints and leaves the
aircraft without control.

Eight visual materials use Gazebo/Purple with explicit ambient, diffuse,
specular, and emissive settings. Mesh references use
model://purple_target/meshes/ instead of model://emir-gazebo-plane2/meshes/.
The libForceVisual.so color settings remain red. That plugin is absent
from this installation. If installed later, its force arrows must not
match the purple target detector.

This file is parsed as XML. Escape angle brackets in description text.
An invalid model.config prevents model://purple_target from loading, so the
ArduPilot connection on port 9012 and its heartbeat never appear.
Keep the external iq_sim source model unchanged.
  </description>
</model>
